<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
                        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
        SEC-04 in docs/security-20260805-0936.md: retiring passwords that predate the 12-character floor.

        Raising a minimum length only governs passwords set after it. Everything already stored keeps
        whatever it was — and bcrypt hashes carry no length, so there is no query that finds the short
        ones. The only thing that can be said with certainty is which accounts existed before the rule
        did, so that is what this marks.

        Set by migration, cleared by a successful password change, and never set again: a one-way flag,
        not a policy engine. While it is set the account can read its own profile and change its
        password, and nothing else (PasswordChangeRequiredFilter).
    -->
    <changeSet id="20260824120000-1" author="jhipster">
        <addColumn tableName="jhi_user">
            <column name="must_change_password" type="boolean" defaultValueBoolean="false">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </addColumn>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
        Every account that exists at this point was created under the old rules. `admin` is the one
        exception, and deliberately so: its password is not chosen by a person. infra.sh generates it
        with `openssl rand` during server bootstrap, and AdminPasswordInitializer rewrites the account
        from ANKOBRA_ADMIN_PASSWORD at every boot. Forcing a change there would be undone by the next
        restart, which would leave the flag permanently set and the account permanently locked to the
        password screen — the failure mode this is supposed to prevent, aimed at the only account that
        can fix it.

        context="prod" for the same reason the SEC-16 removal carries it: this is a data migration for
        accounts real people are using, and the only account it would touch anywhere else is the
        generator-seeded `user`, which the dev and test contexts deliberately keep. Flagging that one
        would lock `@WithMockUser` — whose default username is "user" — out of the entire API, turning
        several hundred integration tests red for a reason none of them is about.

        The dev demo logins are unaffected either way: DataSeeder creates `kojo` and `ama` after
        Liquibase has run, so they get the column default of false. Their published demo1234 password is
        under the floor and stays that way — the demo is meant to log straight in. To exercise the flow
        locally, set the flag by hand, or call the admin endpoint:
        `update jhi_user set must_change_password = true where login = 'kojo';`

        On a production database built from scratch this is a no-op: `admin` is the only row, and it is
        excluded.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20260824120000-2" author="jhipster" context="prod">
        <comment>Flag every pre-existing account as carrying a password that predates the 12-character policy</comment>
        <update tableName="jhi_user">
            <column name="must_change_password" valueBoolean="true"/>
            <where>login != 'admin'</where>
        </update>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
